  "slug": "quantum-error-correction-4176",
  "summary": "Eight November 2026 preprints pair superconducting-hardware error-correction demonstrations with noise-aware calibration, mitigation, simulation and resource-prediction tools.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is confronting realistic, non-ideal noise in error-correcting and error-mitigated quantum computation on superconducting devices. Experimental work scales surface-code stability tests to 7x7 patches on a 105-qubit processor and identifies temporally correlated heating-to-|3> leakage, while magic state cultivation is implemented with lattice-surgery escapes on IBM hardware. Supporting methods include syndrome-based recalibration via gate-error amplification (GEARS), logical-error prediction and geometry selection for rectangular surface codes under biased noise, and Ellis, a Pauli-frame simulator handling qubit loss and leakage. Complementary NISQ-oriented items extend bnZNE mitigation to randomized circuits and propose a noise-aware testing oracle with controlled false-positive rate. The common novelty is coupling characterization of noise structure to decoding, calibration, and resource choices.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
